dict(section="Q2 · The tokenizer",
     title="Idea · What a subword tokenizer does",
     kind="text",
     bullets=["A model reads numbers, not letters: text → pieces → ids from a fixed vocabulary",
              "Common words stay whole; rarer words are cut into smaller known pieces",
              "XLM-R: SentencePiece unigram, ~250k pieces; a word-start piece carries a marker",
              "MuRIL uses WordPiece: pieces that continue a word begin with ##",
              "If nothing in the vocabulary fits → the unknown token, UNK"],
     narration="A neural network cannot read letters. It needs numbers. A tokenizer is the step that turns text into numbers. It has a fixed list of pieces, called the vocabulary, and each piece has an id number. The tokenizer cuts a tweet into pieces from that list, and the model receives the list of ids. Why pieces and not whole words? A vocabulary of every possible word would be enormous and would still miss new words. A vocabulary of single letters would be small, but every tweet would become very long. Subword pieces are the middle path. Common words stay whole, and rarer words are cut into smaller pieces that the vocabulary does know. XLM-R uses a SentencePiece unigram model, with a vocabulary of about two hundred and fifty thousand pieces. It treats the space as part of the text, so a piece that begins a new word carries a small start-of-word marker. MuRIL uses WordPiece, the method from BERT. There, the first piece of a word looks normal, and every piece that continues the same word starts with two hash signs. If no piece fits at all, the tokenizer outputs a special unknown token.",),

