dict(section="Q4 · Fertility",
     title="Idea · Why lower fertility is not automatically better",
     kind="text",
     bullets=["MuRIL: LOWER fertility (1.313) but HIGHER UNK rate (2.196%)",
              "XLM-R: higher fertility (1.401) but almost no UNK (0.006%)",
              "An UNK is one token where real pieces might have been several",
              "So fewer pieces can mean lost information, not a better split",
              "Fertility measures length; it does not measure meaning kept"],
     narration="A tempting conclusion is: MuRIL has lower fertility, so MuRIL's tokenizer is better for Urdu. Be careful. Fertility only counts pieces. It does not ask whether the pieces kept the meaning. Look at both numbers side by side. MuRIL has lower fertility, 1.313, but a much higher unknown rate, 2.196 percent. XLM-R has higher fertility, 1.401, but almost no unknown tokens. Here is why that matters. When a tokenizer cannot represent something, it writes one unknown token. A tokenizer that could represent the same text might need several real pieces for it. So unknown tokens can make the count of pieces smaller, and fertility lower, while the information behind them is gone. Fewer pieces is good only when the pieces still carry the text. That is why the handout asks for both numbers, and why a good answer always quotes them together. Fertility tells you about length and cost. The unknown rate tells you about information lost. Neither one alone tells you which model will classify better.",),

